## Describe the bug
The specific difference in behavior I found is around validating containers (list, maps, optionals...) without adding `@Valid` to them. See the reproducer or the code below.

### Expected behavior
The injected bean validator has the same behavior and supports the same features as the Hibernate default one, in this case, elements in containers are validated without having to explicitly add `@Valid` to them.

### Actual behavior
The injected bean validator behaves differently that the Hibernate default one, in this case it doesn't find any violations on containers unless `@Valid` is explicitly added to them.

## To Reproduce
[container-validation-test.zip](https://github.com/quarkusio/quarkus/files/6539572/container-validation-test.zip) is a maven project that shows the issue, but the only dependency is `quarkus-hibernate-validator` and the whole Java code is this:

```java
@QuarkusMain
public class App implements QuarkusApplication {

    @Inject
    Validator validator1;

    public static void main(String... args) {
        Quarkus.run(App.class, args);
    }

    @Override
    public int run(String... args) {
        final var validator2 = Validation.buildDefaultValidatorFactory().getValidator();

        final var testObject = new DefaultTestObject("");
        final var testContainers = new DefaultTestContainers(Map.of("", ""), List.of(testObject), Optional.of(-1));

        System.out.println(validator1.validate(testContainers).size()); // prints 0
        System.out.println(validator2.validate(testContainers).size()); // prints 3

        return 0;
    }

    static class DefaultTestContainers {
        private final Map<String, String> map;

        private final List<DefaultTestObject> list;

        private final Optional<Integer> optional;

        public DefaultTestContainers(Map<String, String> map, List<DefaultTestObject> list, Optional<Integer> optional) {
            this.map = map;
            this.list = list;
            this.optional = optional;
        }

        // With the container level @Valid annotations (which shouldn't be necessary)
        // both validators print 3

        // @Valid
        public Map<String, @NotBlank String> getMap() {
            return map;
        }

        // @Valid
        public List<@Valid DefaultTestObject> getList() {
            return list;
        }

        // @Valid
        public Optional<@Positive Integer> getOptional() {
            return optional;
        }
    }

    static class DefaultTestObject {
        private final String property;

        DefaultTestObject(String property) {
            this.property = property;
        }

        @NotBlank
        public String getProperty() {
            return property;
        }
    }

}
``` 

